Thank You Nicholas for the inspiring  talk I'm Skylar Tibbits I'm faculty in  the department of architecture and I'm  going to introduce the programmable  session so as we just heard Nikolas  Nikolas predicted that in 1995 the world  would be digital and that obviously has  come true and he predicted many things  that have have come from that but I  would argue the world has become more  and more material in it and I'm going to  try to show you what I mean by that so  this is the first CAD tool Sutherland  sketchpad invented here at MIT that has  its contemporary counterparts in  sophisticated software and simulation  that have radically changed nearly every  discipline whether that's design  mathematics computer science biology  earth and planetary science software and  digital technologies have radically  changed the way we think they radically  change the way we design what we can  imagine what we can optimize evaluate  and ultimately what we can build this is  the first CNC machine also invented here  at MIT in roughly the same time the  first time a computer was connected to a  milling machine and that obviously has  its contemporary counterparts in digital  fabrication if you look at just one of  those sectors if you look at 3d printing  there's nearly everyday new types of  printing from FDM to SLA to SLS and more  and more they're actually bringing us  closer to materials you can look at  Neary's glass printing or  electrospinning clothing or wood  printing or carbon fiber and fiberglass  printing these digital technologies are  not taking us further from reality  they're creating a renewed interest in  physical material properties and we can  see this at a number of different scales  we can look at the smallest of scales  perhaps the at-home DIY maker community  of growing community of people that are  enabled by digital technologies whether  that's 3d printers or digital  fabrication or AR VR or Arduino Zoar  you name it new capabilities that are  creating an interest in questioning how  we make things questioning material  properties inventing new processes to  make and we'll see some of that from  Nadia in a bid you can look at the other  sector of the industrial fabrication and  manufacturing sector and obviously  digital technologies have enabled us to  make things in new ways they've enabled  us to radically rethink material  performance and physical physical  properties but maybe most interesting is  the research sector you can look at  synthetic biology material science bio  printing organ printing and I think all  of these are pointing to the fact that  there's truly a materials revolution  happening enabled by the digital  revolution and it's not that these are  just arguing for the separation of  digital and physical technologies and  now they're coming back together or we  can easily talk between digital and  physical it's literally that the  physical is becoming digital physical  and digital are blending so what I mean  by that is literally we can now program  materials to sense actuate and have  logic in this case the material sensing  light and temperature it can sense  moisture it can sense all sorts of  triggers to become an actuator to fold  curl stretch shrink squirm swim you can  literally embed codes in physical  materials to transform into the letters  MIT or sophisticated and complicated  geometries you can embed programs so  those materials are sensing their  environment responding and then enacting  that program into physical form we can  also embed instructions for materials to  assemble themselves so these can go from  arbitrary structures into furniture we  can go beyond assembly into lifelike  property so they can assemble they can  grow they can encapsulate they can  divide they can grow and divide grow and  divide like cellular mitosis we can show  replication not just in robotic  mechanisms not just in biological  materials but we can show replication  and physical dumb simple materials that  are shaking on a table in a lab I think  all of these demonstrate that physical  materials are not just responding to  digital  properties and not just something that  we make through digital fabrication but  that they have digital properties  embedded in them and we can now program  them just like we did computers and  machines and yesterday we programmed  computers and machines but I believe  today we're programming matter itself so  that's exactly what this panel is about  this panel is called programmable  because we're really rethinking  programmable from software and how we  are interfacing the programmable  technology to digital fabrication to  embedding programs literally in  materials and thinking about the context  across different disciplines so we have  an amazing line-up of speakers in this  session and I'll introduce them briefly  the first is Ben Frey and Casey Reese  Casey is a professor at UCLA and a  computational artist who co-developed  processing with Ben Frey while they were  at the Media Lab processing is an  open-source programming language and a  sketchbook sketchbook for the visual  arts Ben Frey is the principal fathom  here in Boston and best known for his  work in data visualization and  information design next we'll have Manu  Prakash Manu is an assistant professor  of bioengineering at Stanford Manu is a  scientist and physicist working at the  intersection of physical biology and  computing and he's best known for his  work on bubble logic water computing in  the folda scope dollar microscope that  can be folded from paper  Benjamin Bratton is a professor of  visual arts and the director of the  Center of design and geopolitics at UC  San Diego  benjamin is an architect and design  theorist best known for his theories on  global computation and algorithmic  governance and Nadia Peake is a  postdoctoral researcher at MIT Center  for bits and atoms and Nadia is best  known for her work on machines that make  machines and object-oriented Hardware  last but not least our moderator for  this session is Kevin Slavin Kevin is an  assistant professor of media arts and  sciences and the head of the playful  systems group at the MIT Media Lab and  with that I'll hand it over to Kevin  thanks so much  you
 

